-## Startup choices (A5)
-
-**Dependency assurance.** `ALL` challenges every new external library before its commitment point. `MATERIAL` challenges a library only through another I3.1 surface it affects, such as a persistent format, a trust boundary, the primary toolchain, or a reversal requiring data migration, cross-component changes or a broken interface; a library that becomes load-bearing is therefore still challenged before reversal becomes expensive. New external services are always covered. A new project adds many libraries early, so this choice largely sets early assurance volume.
-
-**Review timing.** No deadline is inherited. Choose per-request and stage durations that match how long one round actually takes on your channel. For reference only: the source framework used 180 seconds per request and 600 per stage for an automated harness, and the validation fixture uses 3,600 and 14,400 for hand-carried review. A hand-carried channel may use longer explicit durations; it does not get an unbounded stage.
-
-**Standing Design Discretion.** The proposal is in `governance/templates/STANDING_DESIGN_DISCRETION.md`; framework adoption does not adopt it. Without a covering scope, C5.3 makes every externally observable choice a project property, so incidental details such as message wording or default layout either go to the Operator under I2.3 or become provisional rows under I4. Choose `ADOPT` to let agents settle cheaply reversible incidental behavior under D.10, `NARROW` to limit it, or `DECLINE` when the Operator will specify observable behavior directly. Update DAI-ENVELOPE-01 to match: set `source` to `discretion_source` and `setup_disposition` to the choice; for `ADOPT` or `NARROW`, set `status` to `SETTLED` and `scope_source` to the actual D.9 scope; for `DECLINE`, keep `status` as `PROPOSED` and record the decline in its notes.
-
-## Entrypoints (A6)
-
-Root `AGENTS.md` must contain the block fixed in Activation Manifest A6, byte-for-byte. Add project commands and conventions around it or in `PROJECT_CONVENTIONS.md`. If a root `CLAUDE.md` exists, it must import `AGENTS.md` and the Constitution, agent rules and role overlays, and every import must resolve. Changing the block or the required imports is a governance amendment because A6 lives in a hashed member. Keep root `AGENTS.md` small; Codex shares a default 32 KiB instruction budget across the `AGENTS.md` files it discovers, and the check warns at three-quarters of it.
-
-## Hash and amendment workflow
-
-`check` compares current bytes with the member list and, when adopted, the separately accepted member hashes and accepted-record digest. It never writes. `write-hashes` is only for an unadopted candidate and refuses when any acceptance record is present. It cannot repair accepted drift.
-
-For an amendment, preserve the live accepted record and prepare an isolated proposal copy. Use `candidate-hashes --output <new-file>` to write a separately named candidate hash list without replacing the active one. Only explicit acceptance of the new exact content permits updating the active member list and pointer. A mismatch calls for source reconciliation under C9.10; record it and hold affected reliance, not the whole project. If only local line endings changed, restore the accepted LF bytes from the trusted source and recheck; do not accept different bytes merely to clear the error.
-
-Git users receive LF attributes scoped to governed text files; binary files are never marked as text. Existing repositories may need a deliberate normalization step before first adoption; do not blanket-renormalize unrelated work. Non-Git users preserve UTF-8 and LF through their editor or export settings. Content hashes cover exact bytes.
-
-## Records and concurrency
-
-The manifest is the single record-location map, grouped under the three homes of C9.6. New record identifiers use UUID4 so parallel creators need no shared sequence counter, and each record carries a short title so people can recognize it. The DAI and mission indexes point to directories of separate records; `framework.py index` renders them as readable tables and rejects malformed rows. Ambiguous Operator remarks are preserved verbatim under `project_records/decisions/operator_input/` (D.3), never asked back to the Operator (I2.7). The mission duty holder serializes its JSONL appends; workers retain event facts in their own evidence records for that append. There is no global lock service. `check-events` validates logs against the schema embedded in the hashed telemetry document. No runtime aggregation service is implemented.
-
-## Prior governance
-
-For a project with prior governance, reconcile precedence and working controls before replacement. For a genuinely new project, no fictitious prior constitution, migration record or legacy decision is required. A project not yet under version control satisfies I6.6 with destination and content identity.
+After adoption, never repair drift by regenerating active hashes. Work on a separately identified candidate. Acceptance identifies exact new content. Operational timing and first-mission pointer changes remain outside governing identity but inside the applicable authority envelope. No local structural tool authenticates human permission or enforces runtime authority.
